1724 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3061 0 R
/E (So instead of committing to one segmentation, you hand the parser several ranked segmentations bundled in a lattice, and it picks whichever segmentation yields the best parse. Segmentation and parsing get decided together rather than in a lossy pipeline.)
/Pg 147 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1725 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 819 0 R
/T (Multilingual Issues: Morphology)
/E (Multilingual Issues: Morphology)
/Pg 148 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1726 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3062 0 R
/E (In many languages a single "word" is built from several pieces called morphemes, a stem plus attached grammatical bits : and the word's meaning is the combination of those pieces. )
/Pg 148 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1727 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3063 0 R
/E (So a word isn't atomic; it's stem + morphemes, and the syntax can depend on the morphemes inside a word, not just the word as a whole. )
/Pg 148 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
